      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 508,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, to humanity and all life on Earth, water is much more than a chemical formula. Here is a breakdown of what water is, why it is unique, and why it is so important.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical Properties (The \"Weird\" Science of Water)\nWater is one of the most unique and anomalous substances in the universe. Under normal conditions (room temperature and pressure), it is a liquid, but it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct states:\n*   **Liquid:** The water we drink, swim in, and flow in rivers.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water, found in glaciers, snow, and ice cubes.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Invisible water vapor in the air and steam from boiling water.\n\n**Other remarkable properties include:**\n*   **The Universal Solvent:** Because of its chemical structure, water can dissolve more substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals) than any other liquid. This is how our blood transports nutrients through our bodies.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This is why oceans help regulate the climate of the entire planet, and why coastal areas have milder temperatures than inland areas.\n*   **Ice Floats:** Almost all liquids shrink and become denser when they freeze, but water actually *expands* and becomes less dense. This is why ice floats on top of lakes instead of freezing them solid from the bottom up, allowing marine life to survive the winter.\n*   **Surface Tension:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion). This creates a \"skin\" on the surface of water, which allows insects like water striders to walk across it and enables water to travel up the roots of giant trees.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\nWater covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**, primarily in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water). \n\n*   **Saltwater:** ~96.5% of Earth's water is in the oceans"
